ID  - EBSCO:edseee.11501010
AN  - EBSCO:edseee.11501010
N1  - record_id: EBSCO:edseee.11501010
TI  - Zero Trust Architectures in Cloud Networks: Enhancing Security Across Global Enterprises
AU  - Singh, Bhupendra
AU  - Najana, Madhavi
AU  - Prabhat, Subh
AU  - Sharma, Nandan
AU  - Penumarthi, Venkata
AU  - Ranjan, Piyush
PY  - 2025
JO  - 2025 International Conference on Business Intelligence for Technology Innovation (ICBITI), Business Intelligence for Technology Innovation (ICBITI), 2025 International Conference on
AB  - The growing usage of cloud computing among worldwide organizations has brought tremendous security issues, calling for a shift from the conventional perimeter-centric defenses to more effective, adaptive security models. Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA) provides an encouraging model by imposing ongoing authentication, least privilege access, and micro-segmentation to combat insider threats, unauthorized access, and data breaches. In this paper, a Zero Trust-based Security Framework (ZTSF) for cloud networks has been proposed by incorporating AI-driven identity and access management (IAM), policy-driven micro-segmentation, blockchain-secured trust mechanisms, Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA) across multi-cloud setups, and AI-fueled threat intelligence & response. The suggested framework employs machine learning for anomaly detection, behavioral biometrics for authentication, smart contracts for compliance, and Software-Defined Perimeter (SDP) for workload isolation. It is assessed via simulation platforms like Cloud Sim or Mininet in terms of security effectiveness, latency overhead, and resource utilization. Via case studies and comparative study, we show how ZTSF enhances cloud security for businesses, making it resilient to sophisticated cyber threats and yet maintaining efficiency in operations.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - EBSCO:190522438
AN  - EBSCO:190522438
N1  - record_id: EBSCO:190522438
TI  - Artificial Intelligence as the Next Frontier in Cyber Defense: Opportunities and Risks.
AU  - Afolalu, Oladele
AU  - Tsoeu, Mohohlo Samuel
PY  - 2025
JO  - Electronics (2079-9292)
AB  - The limitations of conventional rule-based security systems have been exposed by the quick evolution of cyber threats, necessitating more proactive, intelligent, and flexible solutions. In cybersecurity, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative factor, offering improved threat detection, prediction, and automated response capabilities. This paper explores the advantages of using AI in strengthening cybersecurity, focusing on its applications in machine learning, Deep Learning, Natural Language Processing, and reinforcement learning. We highlight the improvement brought by AI in terms of real-time incident response, detection accuracy, scalability, and false positive reduction while processing massive datasets. Furthermore, we examine the challenges that accompany the integration of AI into cybersecurity, including adversarial attacks, data quality constraints, interpretability, and ethical implications. The study concludes by identifying potential future directions, such as integration with blockchain and IoT, Explainable AI and the implementation of autonomous security systems. By presenting a comprehensive analysis, this paper underscores exceptional potential of AI to transform cybersecurity into a field that is more robust, adaptive, and predictive.
ER  - 

