AN  - EBSCO:edssjs.CDAA268B
N1  - record_id: EBSCO:edssjs.CDAA268B
TI  - Advancing multi-cloud: an efficient crypto strategy for securing unstructured information distribution
AU  - Ranjan, Vivek
AU  - Raichura, Harshit
AU  - Singh, Preetjot
AU  - Sohal, Jagmeet
AU  - Kavitha, R.
AU  - Yadav, Surendra
PY  - 2024
JO  - International Journal of System Assurance Engineering and Management
AB  - The goal of this research is to enhance security in the multi-cloud storage solution by put forward a new cryptographic method for the unstructured data. The objective is to protect information and its integrity while also reducing threats from infringement and insecure document submissions. Multi-cloud environments are intricate solutions that involve the integration of more than one cloud storage solution to improve data processing and confront massive security challenges. They include protection against unauthorized access, secure uploading of documents, and data integrity in distributed storage systems. To tackle these problems, this study adopts the Elliptic-Blow Fused Cryptography (EbFC) technique that combines the use of elliptic curve cryptography and Blowfish encryption. This approach offers good assurance to risks and evils which might occur in the future. To evaluate the EbFC technique we use several file formats from YouTube in SUSID (Secure Unstructured Information Distribution). The study shows that the proposed EbFC technique has the potentialities of reducing the effects of both malicious user or intruder that are capable of operating unsafe information exchange over the multi-cloud environments. SUSID framework has achieved 100% overall security feature with higher rating in encryption processing time EPT and decryption processing time DPT. These outcomes illustrate the effectiveness of the use of the proposed EbFC method and suggest that the future studies should attempt further development of the algorithm and explore whether it could work with different types of data and cloud environment.<br />
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - EBSCO:188678739
AN  - EBSCO:188678739
N1  - record_id: EBSCO:188678739
TI  - A Secure-by-Design Approach to Big Data Analytics Using Databricks and Format-Preserving Encryption.
AU  - Lagos-Obando, Juan
AU  - Aillapán, Gabriel
AU  - Fenner-López, Julio
AU  - Bustamante-Mora, Ana
AU  - Burgos-López, María
PY  - 2025
JO  - Applied Sciences (2076-3417)
AB  - Managing and analyzing data in data lakes for big data environments requires robust protocols to ensure security, scalability, and compliance with privacy regulations. The increasing need to process sensitive data emphasizes the relevance of secure-by-design approaches that integrate encryption techniques and governance frameworks to protect personal and confidential information. This study proposes a protocol that combines the capabilities of Databricks and format-preserving encryption to improve data security and accessibility in data lakes without compromising usability or structure. The protocol was developed using a design science methodology, incorporating findings from a systematic literature review and validated through expert feedback and proof-of-concept experiments in banking environments. The proposed solution integrates multiple layers, data ingestion, persistence, access, and consumption, leveraging the processing capabilities of Databricks and format-preserving encryption to enable secure data management and governance. Validation results indicate the protocol is effectiveness in protecting sensitive data, with promising applicability in regulated industries. This work contributes to addressing key challenges in big data security and lays the groundwork for future developments in data governance and encryption techniques.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - EBSCO:edssjs.1BE1BB72
AN  - EBSCO:edssjs.1BE1BB72
N1  - record_id: EBSCO:edssjs.1BE1BB72
TI  - Cloud outsourcing: a multigroup analysis of cloud risk and governance effectiveness of EU, US, and Canadian financial institutions
AU  - Anderson-Princen, Jamelia M.
PY  - 2025
JO  - Risk Management
AB  - One of the most important challenges facing financial institutions that outsource cloud technologies is the ability to devise efficient control structures. For this study, I surveyed senior cloud risk experts at large financial institutions in the US, the EU, and Canada to capture their concerns regarding 37 different risks in outsourcing. I employed a multigroup model to test for transaction misalignment and the overall degree of efficiency in managing risks. Empirical results reveal that institutional risk is a primary risk factor, and that all groups face similar risk and governance challenges, even in the face of divergent legal regimes and institutional practices. Additionally, the results showed that US institutions were among the most inefficient in the governance process. These findings indicate that hybrid transactions are difficult to govern owing to the incomplete nature of cloud contracts, the fragmented nature of the regulation, and the presence of institutional risk.<br />
ER  - 

