ID  - EBSCO:edssjs.D543E3C8
AN  - EBSCO:edssjs.D543E3C8
N1  - record_id: EBSCO:edssjs.D543E3C8
TI  - Multi-cloud security model: establishment of inter-server communication for authentication integrity
AU  - Kaur, Sumeet
AU  - Khare, Mayank Deep
AU  - Bhatt, Mukul
AU  - Haripriya, V.
AU  - Kumar, Ajay
AU  - Singla, Abhishek
PY  - 2024
JO  - International Journal of System Assurance Engineering and Management
AB  - Safety measures are used in multi-cloud security to protect infrastructure, apps, and data that are spread across several cloud environments. It necessitates lowering the risks connected to dispersed data access and storage by maintaining honesty and secrecy across several cloud platforms. The purpose of this research is to develop a novel inter-server communication for securing a multi-cloud environment. We discuss several challenges associated with utilizing many clouds and offering solutions such as the Supersingular isogeny Diffie–Hellman (SIDH) algorithm to prevent sensitive messages from being intercepted during internal server communications, Attribute-based encryption with outsourced decryption (ABE-OD) for fine-grained access control, rekeying at user withdrawal, Blowfish for message encryption, Rivest, Shamir, and Adleman (RSA) for key encryption. Our suggested paradigm includes anexchange of keys methodology to strengthen server-to-server prerequisites for communications in addition to security for fine-grained data security and access control on systems for storing. The RSA technique is used in the suggested concept to encrypt secret keys, allowing the encrypted keys to be stored in the database proxy server. The computation times for the construction of the BLAKE2 hash, data encryption, data decryption, and SIDH key exchange were determined. We made use of many parameters, including hash generation time, encryption time, and decryption time. The outcomes of the parameters are hash generation time of 2 mb (45), 4 mb (167), 8 mb (301), 16 mb (421), 22 mb (563), 28 mb (685), 32 mb (705), 36 mb (911), 42 mb (1027), encryption times of 2 mb (24), 4 mb (90), 8 mb (170), 16 mb (265), 22 mb (342), 28 mb (420), 32 mb (508), 36 mb (595), 42 mb (712), and decryption time of 2 mb (42), 4 mb (164), 8 mb (298), 16 mb (417), 22 mb (560), 28 mb (682), 32 mb (705), 36 mb (907), 42 mb (1024). Based on the inter-server communication system, the experiment results demonstrate the effectiveness of the recommended methodology in securing a multi-cloud environment.<br />
